</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.3 An observable two-stage estimator ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is fully observable given a feature map <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>:
it requires only computing Gram/covariance statistics of demonstration representations.
If it returns <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">infeasible target</span>, then the requested floor <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the current lower confidence bound <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that the chosen target is not supported at confidence <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\xi</annotation></semantics></math> under the current feature map and ridge level; practical remedies and calibration guidance are given in Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Theoretical Constants: Sources, Mappings, and Practical Calibration ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>.
The appendix contains a complete non-asymptotic analysis showing how to choose <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>
(and how to split <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>) so that the returned <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}</annotation></semantics></math> attains the target guarantee in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Proposition 3.3 (Spectral coverage from 𝐾 demonstrations). ‣ 3.2 Sample complexity bound ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)
under the same assumptions as Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Spectral coverage from 𝐾 demonstrations). ‣ 3.2 Sample complexity bound ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Both stages operate solely on the demonstration pool (or training split) and do not access
test-query outputs or labels; the resulting prompt length is fixed prior to evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Spectral floor as stability proxy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proxy in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Intuition and example. ‣ 3.1 Problem setup and proxy objective ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is purely geometric: it asks whether the demonstration features span the space
well enough so that the empirical second moment is well-conditioned.
This section provides a modeling rationale—via ridge-regularized linearization—for using such a spectral “floor” as a surrogate for ICL stability, and clarifies what the proxy does <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and does not</em> capture.
We complement the theory with empirical diagnostics in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Interpretability of the Proxy ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> and direct stability measurements in Appendix <a href="#A12" title="Appendix L Direct Measurement of Distributional Stability (Definition 3.1) ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">L</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Many analyses of ICL posit that, for a fixed query <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model’s output depends on demonstrations primarily through a
low-dimensional statistic built from representations (e.g., attention-weighted averages, or a fitted linear probe).
A generic abstraction is:
(i) the prompt induces a parameter estimate <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{w}</annotation></semantics></math> by solving a regularized least-squares problem in feature space,
and (ii) the model output at the query is a Lipschitz function of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under this view, stability reduces to controlling how much <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{w}</annotation></semantics></math> varies when we resample the demonstrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the feature matrix defined in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem setup and proxy objective ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Consider the ridge estimator</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{w}(S_{K})\;:" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{w}(S_{K})\;:</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;\arg\min_{w\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\;\frac{1}{K}\|H_{K}w-y\|_{2}^{2}+\rho\|w\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;\arg\min_{w\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\;\frac{1}{K}\|H_{K}w-y\|_{2}^{2}+\rho\|w\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;\big(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho}\big)^{-1}\,\Big(\frac{1}{K}H_{K}^{\top}y\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;\big(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho}\big)^{-1}\,\Big(\frac{1}{K}H_{K}^{\top}y\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes supervision associated with the demonstrations (e.g., class labels under a fixed
encoding, or pseudo-labels produced by the model itself), and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is the same ridge level as in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Intuition and example. ‣ 3.1 Problem setup and proxy objective ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why spectral coverage implies stability.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At a high level, the smallest eigenvalue of the empirical second-moment matrix
<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}\phi(x_{i})\phi(x_{i})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}\phi(x_{i})\phi(x_{i})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>
quantifies how well the representation vectors of the demonstrations span the feature space.
When <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> is small, the induced inverse geometry is poorly conditioned:
small perturbations in the empirical moments—arising from changing or resampling a few demonstrations—can be greatly amplified,
leading to large variations in the fitted coefficients and, consequently, in the model’s predictions.
In contrast, a larger spectral lower bound ensures that all directions in representation space are sufficiently covered,
which stabilizes the inverse problem and suppresses amplification effects.
This intuition extends to quantile-based spectral surrogates, which trade off strict worst-case control
for improved numerical robustness in finite samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Spectral floor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> bounded sensitivity of ridge parameters)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|y\|_{2}\leq B\sqrt{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>K</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|y\|_{2}\leq B\sqrt{K}</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely for some <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be two independent <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-demo prompts with feature matrices <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{K},H^{\prime}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{K},H^{\prime}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
and ridge covariances <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho},\widehat{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{K,\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho},\widehat{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{K,\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the event</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\delta}\;:=\;\Big\{\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho})\geq\delta\Big\}\;\cap\;\Big\{\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{K,\rho})\geq\delta\Big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\delta}\;:=\;\Big\{\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho})\geq\delta\Big\}\;\cap\;\Big\{\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{K,\rho})\geq\delta\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the ridge solutions satisfy the deterministic bound</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widehat{w}(S_{K})-\widehat{w}(S^{\prime}_{K})\|_{2}\;\leq\;\frac{2B}{\delta}\;+\;\frac{B}{\delta^{2}}\,\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}-\widehat{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{K}\|\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widehat{w}(S_{K})-\widehat{w}(S^{\prime}_{K})\|_{2}\;\leq\;\frac{2B}{\delta}\;+\;\frac{B}{\delta^{2}}\,\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}-\widehat{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{K}\|\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, conditioning improves as the spectral floor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> increases.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From ridge sensitivity to distributional stability (sketch).</span>
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Spectral floor ⇒ bounded sensitivity of ridge parameters). ‣ Why spectral coverage implies stability. ‣ 3.4 Spectral floor as stability proxy ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> shows that a spectral floor
<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho})\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho})\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> prevents the ridge inverse from amplifying
prompt-to-prompt variability in the induced ridge parameter <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under a mild Lipschitz dependence of the model output distribution on <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{w}</annotation></semantics></math>,
this translates into small divergence between <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\cdot\mid P_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\cdot\mid P_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> across independently resampled prompts,
with probability controlled by the proxy failure level <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.
We provide the full derivation (including the Lipschitz condition, intermediate inequalities, and sharper variants
that avoid explicit <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}-\widehat{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{K}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}-\widehat{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{K}\|</annotation></semantics></math> terms) in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Interpretability of the Proxy ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Calibration and practical recommendations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bound in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Spectral coverage from 𝐾 demonstrations). ‣ 3.2 Sample complexity bound ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and the plug-in rule (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 3.3 An observable two-stage estimator ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)
are intentionally conservative: they protect against worst-case directions in the feature space and use
high-probability concentration. Importantly, calibration is introduced as a practical correction for proxy–generator mismatch and finite-sample conservatism; it does not change the non-asymptotic guarantee for the uncalibrated proxy bound, and should be viewed as an empirical tightening step rather than a first-principles refinement of the theory.
In practice, two issues commonly arise:
(i) the smallest eigenvalue is overly sensitive to noise in near-null directions, and
(ii) constants hidden in non-asymptotic inequalities can inflate the suggested prompt length.
This section summarizes lightweight calibration strategies that preserve the monotonic behavior of the proxy
while producing practically useful <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of targeting the extreme floor <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is often more stable to track the quantile eigenvalue
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{q}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{q}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem setup and proxy objective ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Operationally, one may replace the event in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Intuition and example. ‣ 3.1 Problem setup and proxy objective ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) by</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\!\big(\lambda_{q}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho})\geq\delta_{q}\big)\;\geq\;1-\xi," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.090em;" width="1.090em"><mi>Pr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\!\big(\lambda_{q}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K,\rho})\geq\delta_{q}\big)\;\geq\;1-\xi,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for a chosen <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in[0.05,0.2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in[0.05,0.2]</annotation></semantics></math>) and threshold <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This reduces sensitivity to a small number of poorly-covered directions while still enforcing broad spectral coverage.
The same two-stage estimator (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.3 An observable two-stage estimator ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) applies by replacing <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\lambda}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\lambda}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.3 An observable two-stage estimator ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)
with <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{q}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{q}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3.2 Sample complexity bound ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) with the corresponding quantile margin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the plug-in output from (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 3.3 An observable two-stage estimator ‣ 3 Theory ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
We optionally apply a global calibration factor <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and use</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{cal}}\;:=\;\left\lceil\alpha\,\widehat{K}\right\rceil." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>cal</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{cal}}\;:=\;\left\lceil\alpha\,\widehat{K}\right\rceil.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">A practical alternative (used in our experiments) is to calibrate <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> against empirical knee-points
estimated on training data only (no test leakage; Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Reproducibility Specification ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).
For each encoder–generator pair, we select a small set of calibration tasks and estimate
<math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{knee}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>knee</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{knee}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> on a held-out validation subset drawn from the training split.
We then choose <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\star}\in[0.05,0.2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\star}\in[0.05,0.2]</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the median absolute log-error
between <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{raw}}^{(t)}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>raw</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{raw}}^{(t)}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{knee}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>knee</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{knee}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> across calibration tasks, and set
<math id="S3.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> by a robust median ratio fit:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\;:=\;\mathrm{median}_{t}\ \widehat{K}_{\mathrm{knee}}^{(t)}\big/\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{raw}}^{(t)}(q^{\star})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>median</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>knee</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>raw</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\;:=\;\mathrm{median}_{t}\ \widehat{K}_{\mathrm{knee}}^{(t)}\big/\widehat{K}_{\mathrm{raw}}^{(t)}(q^{\star}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, we freeze <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q^{\star},\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q^{\star},\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> and report both raw and calibrated predictions on disjoint evaluation tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Default practical choices.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Robust default settings (ridge level, spectral threshold choice, and failure-budget split) are summarized in Appendix <a href="#A10.SS5" title="J.5 Default practical choices ‣ Appendix J Theoretical Constants: Sources, Mappings, and Practical Calibration ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>
Error ratio (<math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}/\text{knee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mtext>knee</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}/\text{knee}</annotation></semantics></math>) across datasets and task families for commercial API generators.
Encoder groups a/b/c correspond to <span id="S3.T1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">all-mpnet-base-v2</span>, <span id="S3.T1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span>, and
<span id="S3.T1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">paraphrase-MiniLM-L12-v2</span>. Entries report the mean ratio over <math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> random seeds; per-cell standard deviations
(and per-seed raw outputs) are provided in the released artifact and summarized in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Reproducibility Specification ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.
Knee-points are estimated on the test split via a two-segment piecewise-linear fit on the grid <math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
with bootstrap confidence intervals (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Reproducibility Specification ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).
The Raw column uses the default theoretical constants, whereas Cal. uses validation-only calibration
(Appendix <a href="#A10.SS2" title="J.2 Theoretical Origins and Geometric Interpretation ‣ Appendix J Theoretical Constants: Sources, Mappings, and Practical Calibration ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J.2</span></a>). Lower is better (closer to 1).
</figcaption>
