<td id="A5.T3.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)/\|\Sigma\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)/\|\Sigma\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Effective rank</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Sub-Gaussian parameter</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Target lower bound for <math id="A5.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Constant in operator-norm deviation</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.5.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.5.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime},C^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime},C^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.5.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Constants in plug-in lower bounds</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.5.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.5.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A5.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{K}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.5.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Truncation threshold</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.5.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.5.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A5.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.5.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A5.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-quantile eigenvalue surrogate</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main theorem assumes that feature vectors <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. across prompt positions.
In practice, contextualized representations may exhibit correlations across <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> due to attention and residual connections.
We now extend the analysis under weak dependence assumptions.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Martingale-difference setting</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a martingale-difference sequence with respect to a natural filtration
<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., conditioned on past tokens).
That is, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the sequence of second-moment deviations</p>
<table id="A5.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\;=\;\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}-\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\;=\;\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}-\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a matrix martingale-difference sequence.
By applying the matrix Azuma–Hoeffding inequality, we obtain concentration of
<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\Sigma}_{K}=\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{t=1}^{K}\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\Sigma}_{K}=\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{t=1}^{K}\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> around its expectation,
with variance proxy and tail probability essentially the same as in the i.i.d. case,
up to a constant factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition E.1</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower bound under martingale differences)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption (A4) with bounded martingale differences,
the non-asymptotic lower bound on <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem 3.1 remains valid
up to replacing <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\cdot\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\cdot\xi</annotation></semantics></math> for an absolute constant <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Mixing sequences</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, suppose <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing with coefficient <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(s)\leq\rho^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(s)\leq\rho^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Matrix Bernstein inequalities for mixing sequences yield concentration with an effective sample size</p>
<table id="A5.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{eff}}\;\approx\;\frac{K}{1+c_{\rho}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{eff}}\;\approx\;\frac{K}{1+c_{\rho}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho}=\sum_{s\geq 1}\alpha(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho}=\sum_{s\geq 1}\alpha(s)</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies temporal dependence.
In particular, geometric mixing (<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(s)\leq\rho^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(s)\leq\rho^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>) gives <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho}=O(1/(1-\rho))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho}=O(1/(1-\rho))</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that moderate dependence inflates the required prompt length by at most a constant factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary E.2</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower bound under mixing)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption (A4) with mixing coefficient <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,
the sufficient condition on prompt length becomes</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\gtrsim\;(1+c_{\rho})\cdot\frac{\sigma^{2}\,\|\Sigma\|}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\log\!\frac{d}{\xi}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≳</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ξ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\gtrsim\;(1+c_{\rho})\cdot\frac{\sigma^{2}\,\|\Sigma\|}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\log\!\frac{d}{\xi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho}=O(1/(1-\rho))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho}=O(1/(1-\rho))</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the strength of temporal dependence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kurtosis stays near the Gaussian baseline (3.0) for shorter prompts but increases with <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>,
indicating heavier tails at longer contexts and motivating the heavy-tail extension (A3).
The drift statistic <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(K)</annotation></semantics></math> (defined as the average <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> deviation between contextualized and context-free
representations) grows gradually with <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, suggesting that representation drift accumulates with longer prompts,
consistent with (A2).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A potentially counter-intuitive observation is that <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> can decrease as <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases.
This does <em id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> contradict the nominal i.i.d. picture for two reasons.
First, <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> of a finite-sample second-moment estimator is not monotone in <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in general:
adding new rank-one terms can increase anisotropy and therefore reduce the smallest eigenvalue.
A simple example is when early samples span multiple directions but additional samples concentrate on a dominant direction,
shifting mass toward the top eigenvectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second (and more importantly in our setting), demonstrations are typically <em id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">query-conditioned</em> due to retrieval.
As <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases, newly appended demonstrations are often increasingly redundant with earlier ones (e.g., lower diversity among nearest neighbors),
which can reduce the effective rank and the empirical spectral coverage.
Therefore a downward trend in <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> is an empirical signature of non-i.i.d. selection and mild dependence/drift,
which is precisely why the robustness extensions (A2)–(A4) are relevant.
Notably, the observed trend correlates with the empirical knee-point in ICL accuracy, supporting the use of spectral coverage as a stability diagnostic.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Discussion</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These results show that our main conclusions are <em id="A5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robust to weak dependence</em>.
Even if contextualized features <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are not independent,
as long as dependence decays over time (mixing) or is conditionally centered (martingale-difference),
the sufficient sample size bound degrades only by a multiplicative factor,
rather than failing altogether.
In practice, this suggests that <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> can still serve as a useful stability proxy,
with dependence merely shifting the knee-point moderately to the right.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Extensions Beyond the Nominal Assumption</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main paper we derived our non-asymptotic lower bound under the nominal assumption (A1),
namely that representations <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, i.i.d., and sub-Gaussian with covariance <math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we provide extensions under weaker assumptions (A2)–(A4), complementing Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Robustness Extensions ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Extensions to Dependent Features ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.
Overall, these results show that our theory is <em id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robust</em>:
the sufficient prompt length bound degrades smoothly under moderate violations of (A1),
rather than failing abruptly.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Representation Drift (A2)</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Robustness Extensions ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>,
if the representation depends on the context index <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, modeled as
<math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>,
then eigenvalue perturbation bounds (Weyl’s inequality)
imply that <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced by at most <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Combining this with matrix Bernstein yields a lower bound of the form</p>
<table id="A6.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\geq\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}+g(\varepsilon)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\geq\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}+g(\varepsilon),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> polynomial in <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and spectral ratios.
Hence, small drift only shifts the required prompt length slightly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Relaxed Tail Assumptions (A3)</h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> has heavier tails than sub-Gaussian e.g., only sub-exponential
or bounded fourth moments then truncation and Bernstein’s inequality
give concentration at a slightly slower rate.
In this case, the sufficient prompt length becomes</p>
<table id="A6.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\gtrsim\;\frac{(\|\Sigma\|^{2}+\kappa^{2})}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\mathrm{polylog}(d/\xi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≳</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>polylog</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\gtrsim\;\frac{(\|\Sigma\|^{2}+\kappa^{2})}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\mathrm{polylog}(d/\xi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is a tail parameter depending on the fourth moment or sub-exponential constant.
Thus, the order of the bound remains unchanged, but the constant is inflated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.3 </span>Weak Dependence (A4)</h3>

<div id="A6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When features are not independent across <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,
we can use concentration for martingale-difference sequences
or <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing processes.
Both cases yield an effective sample size of the form
<math id="A6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{eff}}=K/(1+c_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{eff}}=K/(1+c_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dependence factor.
Thus, weak dependence inflates the sufficient prompt length by a multiplicative constant,
but does not alter the overall scaling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.4 </span>Summary of Extensions</h3>

<div id="A6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results under (A2)–(A4) can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="A6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Drift in representations adds an additive correction <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, linear or quadratic in the drift magnitude.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Heavy-tailed features require an inflated constant, governed by moment parameters, but retain the same quadratic dependence on the spectral gap.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Temporal dependence reduces the effective sample size, inflating the required <math id="A6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by a constant factor depending on mixing strength.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, these extensions demonstrate that the main non-asymptotic bound is conservative but stable:
it remains meaningful even when real-world deviations from (A1) occur,
providing a useful guideline for interpreting empirical ICL stability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A6.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="A6.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of assumptions and their effect on the sufficient prompt length <math id="A6.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here <math id="A6.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{nominal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{nominal}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the bound under (A1).</span></figcaption>
<table id="A6.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A6.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A6.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt">
