<section id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kurtosis stays near the Gaussian baseline (3.0) for shorter prompts but increases with <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating heavier tails at longer contexts, consistent with Assumption (A3). The drift statistic <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(K)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as the average <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm difference between contextualized and context-free representations, grows gradually with <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (from 0.05 at <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=16</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.21 at <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math>). This progressive increase suggests that representation drift accumulates as more demonstrations are included, supporting Assumption (A2) with small but non-negligible drift. Crucially, the smooth growth of <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(K)</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the additional sample size required to compensate for drift increases polynomially with the drift magnitude <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (as shown in Theorem <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem D.3 (Robustness under bounded drift (with explicit variance proxy)). ‣ Variance-proxy derivation (i.n.i.d.). ‣ D.1 Robustness to Representation Drift (A2) ‣ Appendix D Robustness Extensions ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>), providing practitioners with a predictable relationship between observed drift and prompt length requirements.
The spectral statistic <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> decreases as <math id="A6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases, and its trend correlates with the empirical knee-point in ICL accuracy.
Overall, these diagnostics support the claim that nominal assumptions are useful approximations in practice, while the relaxed extensions (A2)–(A4) capture systematic deviations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Synthetic Stress Tests</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the robustness of our theoretical bound under controlled violations of Assumption (A1),
we conducted synthetic stress tests along three axes:
(i) bounded drift in representations (A2),
(ii) heavy-tailed features (A3),
and (iii) temporal dependence (A4).
For each setting we generated synthetic features and tasks as described in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Synthetic Stress Tests ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a> (Section <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Synthetic feature and task generation ‣ Appendix G Synthetic Stress Tests ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>),
measured empirical knee-points <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\text{emp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>emp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\text{emp}}</annotation></semantics></math> of in-context learning performance,
and compared them against the theoretical lower bound <math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{nominal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{nominal}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>Synthetic feature and task generation</h3>

<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All stress tests use a controlled synthetic ICL surrogate where the only changing factor is the feature distribution.
We generate a sequence of feature vectors <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z_{t}\}_{t=1}^{K}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{t}\}_{t=1}^{K}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (the “demonstration representations”)
and define a simple supervised task by a random linear teacher.
Specifically, we draw a teacher vector <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and assign labels
<math id="A7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=\mathrm{sign}(\langle w^{\star},z_{t}\rangle+\eta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=\mathrm{sign}(\langle w^{\star},z_{t}\rangle+\eta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> with i.i.d. noise <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrations, the in-context predictor is instantiated by ridge regression on the synthetic features:</p>
<table id="A7.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{w}_{K}\in\arg\min_{w}\sum_{t=1}^{K}(y_{t}-\langle w,z_{t}\rangle)^{2}+\lambda\|w\|_{2}^{2},\qquad\widehat{y}(x)=\mathrm{sign}(\langle\widehat{w}_{K},z(x)\rangle)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>w</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{w}_{K}\in\arg\min_{w}\sum_{t=1}^{K}(y_{t}-\langle w,z_{t}\rangle)^{2}+\lambda\|w\|_{2}^{2},\qquad\widehat{y}(x)=\mathrm{sign}(\langle\widehat{w}_{K},z(x)\rangle).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ACC}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ACC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ACC}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> on a held-out synthetic test set generated from the same setting and estimate
<math id="A7.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\text{emp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>emp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\text{emp}}</annotation></semantics></math> using the same knee-point estimator as in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Reproducibility Specification ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.
Uncertainty in <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\text{emp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>emp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\text{emp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is quantified by percentile bootstrap over test instances (with <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=200</annotation></semantics></math> resamples,
matching Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experimental Reproducibility Specification ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Nominal baseline.</h5>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the nominal setting (A1), we sample <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. with a fixed PSD matrix <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bounded drift (A2).</h5>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To model bounded representation drift, we sample i.i.d. base features <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{z}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{z}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> and add a bounded perturbation</p>
<table id="A7.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=\tilde{z}_{t}+\Delta_{t},\qquad\|\Delta_{t}\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=\tilde{z}_{t}+\Delta_{t},\qquad\|\Delta_{t}\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Unless stated otherwise, we set <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{t}=\varepsilon\,u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{t}=\varepsilon\,u</annotation></semantics></math> with a fixed unit vector <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> (systematic drift), which makes the violation controlled
and reproducible; random-direction drift can be generated by sampling <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly on the unit sphere.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Heavy tails (A3).</h5>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To model heavy-tailed features, we sample <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from a multivariate Student-<math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> distribution with <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> degrees of freedom and rescale it to match
a target second-moment:</p>
<table id="A7.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{t}=\sqrt{\frac{\nu-2}{\nu}}\,\Sigma^{1/2}\,g_{t},\qquad g_{t}\sim t_{\nu}(0,I_{d}),\quad(\nu&gt;2)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>ν</mi></mfrac></msqrt><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=\sqrt{\frac{\nu-2}{\nu}}\,\Sigma^{1/2}\,g_{t},\qquad g_{t}\sim t_{\nu}(0,I_{d}),\quad(\nu&gt;2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Lower <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> yields heavier tails while preserving the same second-moment scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Temporal dependence (A4).</h5>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To introduce dependence while keeping the same marginal second-moment, we use a stationary AR(1) process:</p>
<table id="A7.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=\rho\,z_{t-1}+\sqrt{1-\rho^{2}}\,\varepsilon_{t},\qquad\varepsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=\rho\,z_{t-1}+\sqrt{1-\rho^{2}}\,\varepsilon_{t},\qquad\varepsilon_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[z_{t}z_{t}^{\top}]=\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[z_{t}z_{t}^{\top}]=\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and the correlation strength increases with <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.2 </span>Bounded Drift</h3>

<figure id="A7.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A7.T6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="A7.T6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Drift stress test results (<math id="A7.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=50</annotation></semantics></math>).
<math id="A7.T6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{nominal}}=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{nominal}}=32</annotation></semantics></math> computed by  (<a href="#A1.E23" title="Equation 23 ‣ A.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ Appendix A Complete Theory Chain ‣ Stability of In-Context Learning: A Spectral Coverage Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>).
<math id="A7.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\text{emp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>emp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\text{emp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is reported with 95% bootstrap CI.</span></figcaption>
